# library/reasoning/corpus/extra/eng-ita.dix -- entries Apertium's eng-ita
# bilingual dictionary lacks, in its own shape, one a line. build.pl reads
# them BEFORE the raw dictionary, so an entry both ways is the first meaning
# of its word in its class. Every word here has its paradigm in
# apertium-ita.ita.dix, so the builder writes all its forms; a word the
# monolingual lacks too goes in extra/italian.txt as lesson lines instead.
# A line that is not an entry is a comment.
#
# r="RL" is what nearly every entry wants: the entry is there so that the
# ITALIAN word can be READ, and an entry both ways would come first in its
# class and make `manina' the first Italian word for `hand' and `lieto' the
# first for `happy' -- a regression for every text translated INTO Italian.
# An RL entry is kept only when the dictionary gave the Italian word no
# meaning in that class, and it comes after every entry both ways.
# `orientamento' is both ways on purpose: the dictionary's first meaning for
# it is `guidance', and `la mamma ha perso l'orientamento' is orientation.
#
# the Monte Livata article (Italian UD, ISDT test-232..260)
<e r="RL"><p><l>rescuer<s n="n"/></l><r>soccorritore<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>happy<s n="adj"/></l><r>lieto<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>finding<s n="n"/></l><r>ritrovamento<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>disappear<s n="vblex"/></l><r>sparire<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>locate<s n="vblex"/></l><r>individuare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>speleological<s n="adj"/></l><r>speleologico<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>car<s n="n"/></l><r>auto<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>deem<s n="vblex"/></l><r>reputare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>ski<s n="vblex"/></l><r>sciare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>extinguish<s n="vblex"/></l><r>spegnere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>arouse<s n="vblex"/></l><r>destare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>recover<s n="vblex"/></l><r>riprendere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>joke<s n="vblex"/></l><r>scherzare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>hand<s n="n"/></l><r>manina<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>effective<s n="adj"/></l><r>fattivo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>mount<s n="n"/></l><r>monte<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>canine<s n="adj"/></l><r>cinofilo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>orientation<s n="n"/></l><r>orientamento<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>move<s n="vblex"/></l><r>muovere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>need<s n="vblex"/></l><r>bisognare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>exhausted<s n="adj"/></l><r>provato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>awake<s n="adj"/></l><r>vigile<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>confused<s n="adj"/></l><r>confusionale<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `lanciare' is `back' first in the dictionary, and `l'uomo aveva lanciato
# l'allarme' launched it; both ways, because `launch' is `lanciare' too
<e><p><l>launch<s n="vblex"/></l><r>lanciare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `mettere' is `contact' first and `ricerca' `enquiry': `è stato messo a
# disposizione', `le ricerche dei bambini'. Both ways, as each is the Italian
# for its English word too
<e><p><l>put<s n="vblex"/></l><r>mettere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>search<s n="n"/></l><r>ricerca<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `bosco' is `wood' first, which Spanish writes `madera' (timber): `nei
# boschi' is in the woods. `carabiniere' is `policeman' first, which Spanish
# writes `policía', the force; a Spanish page writes `los carabineros'
<e><p><l>forest<s n="n"/></l><r>bosco<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>carabineer<s n="n"/></l><r>carabiniere<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# `trasportare' is `convey' first and `soccorrere' `help': `è stata
# trasportata in ospedale', `soccorsa dai carabinieri'
<e><p><l>transport<s n="vblex"/></l><r>trasportare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>rescue<s n="vblex"/></l><r>soccorrere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `succedere' is `succeed' first; `come possa essere successo', `è successo
# l'inferno' happened
<e><p><l>happen<s n="vblex"/></l><r>succedere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# the noun `soccorso' has its paradigm and no English: `gli uomini del
# soccorso alpino e speleologico'
<e r="RL"><p><l>rescue<s n="n"/></l><r>soccorso<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# `iniziare' is `start' first, which Spanish writes `arrancar' (an engine):
# `dal punto in cui avevano iniziato la passeggiata'
<e><p><l>begin<s n="vblex"/></l><r>iniziare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `medico' is `medic' first, a word the Spanish dictionary has none for:
# `i medici stanno valutando le loro condizioni'
<e><p><l>doctor<s n="n"/></l><r>medico<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# `entrare' has only `come in' and `get in', which are more than a word, and
# the noun `passeggiata' only its participle's reading: `è entrata nel
# panico', `voleva fare una passeggiata'
<e r="RL"><p><l>enter<s n="vblex"/></l><r>entrare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>walk<s n="n"/></l><r>passeggiata<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `allontanare' is `remove' first: `la madre si è allontanata con i bambini'
# went away from where she was, and took nothing away
<e><p><l>distance<s n="vblex"/></l><r>allontanare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# the noun `scomparsa' has its paradigm and no English: `la storia della
# scomparsa e del ritrovamento di una donna'
<e r="RL"><p><l>disappearance<s n="n"/></l><r>scomparsa<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `raggiungere' is `achieve' first, which Spanish writes `conseguir': `li
# hanno raggiunti', `le temperature hanno raggiunto i -10 gradi' is reaching
<e><p><l>reach<s n="vblex"/></l><r>raggiungere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `primo' is `prime' first: `fin dalle prime ore' is the first hours
<e><p><l>first<s n="adj"/></l><r>primo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `nota' is `grade' first, a school's mark: `fa sapere, in una nota'
<e><p><l>note<s n="n"/></l><r>nota<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `forestale' is `forestry', which is a noun in Spanish: `il Corpo forestale
# dello Stato' is the forest one, `forestal'
<e><p><l>forest<s n="adj"/></l><r>forestale<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# the adjective `disperso' has its paradigm and no English, and read as the
# participle of `disperdere' the woman was dispersed: `la donna dispersa sul
# monte Livata' is missing
<e r="RL"><p><l>missing<s n="adj"/></l><r>disperso<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `altro' has its adjective paradigm and no English in that class, only the
# pronoun and a masculine determiner: `è andata dall'altra' is the other
# one, a noun left out after an article and an adjective
<e><p><l>other<s n="adj"/></l><r>altro<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `volunteer' has no entry, and `volontari' -- the volunteers the region
# thanks -- read as the adjective `voluntary' and was written `voluntario',
# singular
<e><p><l>volunteer<s n="n"/></l><r>volontario<s n="n"/><s n="GD"/></r></p></e>
#
# the Fiat-Chrysler article (Italian UD, ISDT test-261..281: the Veba
# agreement of January 2014), into Spanish. `restante', `rimanente', `miliardo',
# `intesa', `complessivo', `paritetico', `pagabile', `annuo' and `grato' have
# their paradigms and no English: `il restante 41,46% di Chrysler', `per
# 3,6 miliardi', `un Memorandum d'Intesa', `un importo complessivo',
# `quattro quote paritetiche pagabili su base annua', `Sarò per sempre grato'
<e><p><l>remaining<s n="adj"/></l><r>restante<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>remaining<s n="adj"/></l><r>rimanente<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>billion<s n="n"/></l><r>miliardo<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>understanding<s n="n"/></l><r>intesa<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>total<s n="adj"/></l><r>complessivo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>equal<s n="adj"/></l><r>paritetico<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>payable<s n="adj"/></l><r>pagabile<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>annual<s n="adj"/></l><r>annuo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>grateful<s n="adj"/></l><r>grato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `memorandum' and `liquidità' are in the dictionary with a paradigm named
# beside the pair, which the builder reads as no word's own entry
<e r="RL"><p><l>memorandum<s n="n"/></l><r>memorandum<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>liquidity<s n="n"/></l><r>liquidità<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# the English words Italian business prose borrows, with their invariant
# paradigms: `al team di leadership'
<e r="RL"><p><l>team<s n="n"/></l><r>team<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>leadership<s n="n"/></l><r>leadership<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `procedere' has no entry, and the dictionary's first Italian for `proceed'
# is `provenire', to come from: `procedere ad investire'
<e><p><l>proceed<s n="vblex"/></l><r>procedere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `attendere' has no entry: `da cui ci attendiamo positive conseguenze' is
# expected; and `avvenire' has only `take place', more than a word: `il
# pagamento della prima quota avverrà'
<e r="RL"><p><l>expect<s n="vblex"/></l><r>attendere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>happen<s n="vblex"/></l><r>avvenire<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `ulteriore' is `ulterior' first, which in English is a hidden motive:
# `ulteriori contribuzioni' are additional ones
<e><p><l>additional<s n="adj"/></l><r>ulteriore<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `versare' is `pour' first, and a sum paid out is disbursed: `Fiat verserà
# a Veba 3,65 miliardi di dollari', `le tre rimanenti quote saranno versate'
<e><p><l>disburse<s n="vblex"/></l><r>versare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `struttura' is `frame' first, and `alla luce della struttura di
# finanziamento' is the deal's structure; the adverb `ora' has its paradigm
# and no English -- the dictionary gives `now' to `or' -- and `Ora è
# indispensabile procedere' is now
<e><p><l>structure<s n="n"/></l><r>struttura<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>now<s n="adv"/></l><r>ora<s n="adv"/></r></p></e>
# the noun `scelta' has its paradigm and no English, and read as the
# participle of `scegliere' it was chosen: `Una scelta strategica da cui ci
# attendiamo positive conseguenze'
<e><p><l>choice<s n="n"/></l><r>scelta<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# the nouns `impegno' and `importo' have their paradigms and no English, and
# each was read as the verb's first person: `il loro incessante impegno',
# `per un importo complessivo'; and `permettere' is `afford' first, where
# `ci permetterà di realizzare' allows
<e r="RL"><p><l>commitment<s n="n"/></l><r>impegno<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>amount<s n="n"/></l><r>importo<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>allow<s n="vblex"/></l><r>permettere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
#
# the violin exhibition in Valencia, into Italian: `rendere tributo',
# `istintivo', and `virtuoso' is a noun too -- a player, where the
# dictionary gives only the adjective `virtuous'
<e><p><l>render<s n="vblex"/></l><r>rendere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>instinctive<s n="adj"/></l><r>istintivo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>virtuoso<s n="n"/></l><r>virtuoso<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>unpublished<s n="adj"/></l><r>inedito<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `destacó algunas de las diferencias': to highlight is `evidenziare'; the
# dictionary put `distaccare', to detach, first
<e><p><l>highlight<s n="vblex"/></l><r>evidenziare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# a collection of instruments is `collezione', where the dictionary put the
# tax collector's `riscossione' first; a piece is `pezzo' before the music's
# `brano'; a range of goods is `gamma' before the radius, `raggio'
<e><p><l>collection<s n="n"/></l><r>collezione<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>piece<s n="n"/></l><r>pezzo<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>range<s n="n"/></l><r>gamma<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `ninguna sofisticada máquina': the Italian dictionary has `sofisticato'
# and no English for it
<e><p><l>sophisticated<s n="adj"/></l><r>sofisticato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `ofrecerán un concierto' is `offriranno', where the dictionary gives
# `offrire' one way only and `brindare', to toast, the other; `entre sus
# manos' is `tra', where it put the dictionary's odd `entri' first; and
# `las cotizadas piezas' are prized, `pregiato'
<e><p><l>offer<s n="vblex"/></l><r>offrire<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>among<s n="pr"/></l><r>tra<s n="pr"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>prized<s n="adj"/></l><r>pregiato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `anche con misure coercitive': the monolingual dictionary has `coercitivo'
# and the bilingual one no English for it
<e><p><l>coercive<s n="adj"/></l><r>coercitivo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# the Monreale article (Italian UD, VIT-9465..9470): words the monolingual
# dictionary has and the bilingual one gives no English
<e r="RL"><p><l>pocket<s n="vblex"/></l><r>intascare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>swindle<s n="vblex"/></l><r>truffare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>inflate<s n="vblex"/></l><r>gonfiare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>cathedral<s n="n"/></l><r>duomo<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>Norman<s n="adj"/></l><r>normanno<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>Palermitan<s n="adj"/></l><r>palermitano<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>monsignor<s n="n"/></l><r>monsignore<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>accusation<s n="n"/></l><r>accusa<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>restoration<s n="n"/></l><r>restauro<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# the record-market report, from Spanish: `discografico' and `rallentamento'
# have paradigms in apertium-ita and no English here
<e><p><l>discographic<s n="adj"/></l><r>discografico<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>slowdown<s n="n"/></l><r>rallentamento<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# the island-traffic article (Italian UD, VIT-9472..9484): `scatta lo stop',
# `godranno di qualche boccata', `trascorrere le proprie ferie', `in lembi di
# terra', `amene località', `le moto', `è vietato', `le più canicolari Eolie',
# `interdette al traffico', `senza penalizzare' -- each has its paradigm in
# apertium-ita and no English here
<e r="RL"><p><l>start<s n="vblex"/></l><r>scattare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>enjoy<s n="vblex"/></l><r>godere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>spend<s n="vblex"/></l><r>trascorrere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>strip<s n="n"/></l><r>lembo<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>pleasant<s n="adj"/></l><r>ameno<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>motorbike<s n="n"/></l><r>moto<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>forbid<s n="vblex"/></l><r>vietare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>canicular<s n="adj"/></l><r>canicolare<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>prohibit<s n="vblex"/></l><r>interdire<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>penalise<s n="vblex"/></l><r>penalizzare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# the opera review, from Spanish: words apertium-ita has a paradigm for and
# no English here. `aria' and `convincimento' are written only from English
# (r="LR"), after the two-way entries, so the Italian `aria' is still air
# and `convincimento' still conviction when they are read
<e><p><l>stylized<s n="adj"/></l><r>stilizzato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>lord<s n="n"/></l><r>lord<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>vocality<s n="n"/></l><r>vocalità<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>fatuous<s n="adj"/></l><r>fatuo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>intone<s n="vblex"/></l><r>intonare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="LR"><p><l>aria<s n="n"/></l><r>aria<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="LR"><p><l>convincement<s n="n"/></l><r>convincimento<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# the Ferlaino interview (Italian UD, VIT-9540..9566): words apertium-ita has
# a paradigm for and no English here. `scudetto' is the league's title and
# `coca' the drug; each is read only into English
<e r="RL"><p><l>cocaine<s n="n"/></l><r>coca<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>title<s n="n"/></l><r>scudetto<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>addict<s n="n"/></l><r>tossicodipendente<s n="n"/><s n="mf"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>ride<s n="vblex"/></l><r>cavalcare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>finish<s n="n"/></l><r>traguardo<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>misfortune<s n="n"/></l><r>sfortuna<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>designator<s n="n"/></l><r>designatore<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>disastrous<s n="adj"/></l><r>disastrato<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# the Washington Post extract (Italian UD, VIT-9750..9762)
<e r="RL"><p><l>fix<s n="vblex"/></l><r>fissare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>tycoon<s n="n"/></l><r>magnate<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>uncertainty<s n="n"/></l><r>incertezza<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# the Clinton inquiry (AnCora CESS-CAST-P-19981202-34), the words Spanish
# gives it: `second' as an adjective -- Italian's `secondo' was a noun, an
# adverb and a preposition -- `pressure' as a verb, `pressare', and
# `sesiones', which no Italian word meant. Read only into English, as each
# is the one word its English has here
<e r="RL"><p><l>second<s n="adj"/></l><r>secondo<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>pressure<s n="vblex"/></l><r>pressare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e r="RL"><p><l>session<s n="n"/></l><r>sessione<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `la ampliación de la investigación': no Italian word meant `enlargement',
# and `ampliamento' is `expansion' in both ways already, so a one-way entry
# would be dropped for it -- this one goes first both ways; `aforementioned'
# is `suddetto' and an intern is a `stagista', which apertium-ita has and
# the bilingual pairs with nothing
<e><p><l>enlargement<s n="n"/></l><r>ampliamento<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>aforementioned<s n="adj"/></l><r>suddetto<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>intern<s n="n"/></l><r>stagista<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `enviar órdenes de comparecer': to send -- `spedire' has a paradigm in
# apertium-ita and the bilingual pairs it with nothing. Two hand lines in
# italian.txt (the twelve's `aveva spedito un fax') gave `spedisce' and its
# participle and no infinitive, so `spedire' could not be written; this
# entry gives every form, and the two lines are gone
<e><p><l>send<s n="vblex"/></l><r>spedire<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `lo que hace es "desacreditar" la investigación': to discredit --
# apertium-ita has `screditare' and the bilingual pairs it with nothing
<e><p><l>discredit<s n="vblex"/></l><r>screditare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# the letter on the Bosnian holocaust (Italian UD, VIT-9763..9774): words
# apertium-ita has and the bilingual pairs with nothing. `Nazism' is written
# capitalised, as the raw entry for `nazionalsocialismo' is: a lower-case
# `nazism' would stop the builder lowering that one, and it lost its meaning
<e><p><l>Nazism<s n="n"/></l><r>nazismo<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>mediate<s n="vblex"/></l><r>mediare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>cowardly<s n="adj"/></l><r>vigliacco<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>petty<s n="adj"/></l><r>meschino<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>predecessor<s n="n"/></l><r>predecessore<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>mad<s n="adj"/></l><r>folle<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>scoundrel<s n="n"/></l><r>mascalzone<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
<e><p><l>eve<s n="n"/></l><r>vigilia<s n="n"/><s n="f"/></r></p></e>
# `e probabilmente è già tardi': late, the adverb -- apertium-ita has
# `tardi' as one (ieri__adv) and the bilingual pairs it with nothing
<e><p><l>late<s n="adv"/></l><r>tardi<s n="adv"/></r></p></e>
# `l'intero popolo bosniaco': Bosnian, the adjective -- the bilingual pairs
# `bosniaco' only as the noun, so the phrase had two nouns that could not be
# adjectives, and position made `intero' (an integer) its head
<e><p><l>bosnian<s n="adj"/></l><r>bosniaco<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# `el presidente del Gobierno se alegra de que ...': to gladden, and glad
# as the reflexive -- the bilingual pairs `gladden' with `allietare' only,
# which apertium-ita does not carry
<e><p><l>gladden<s n="vblex"/></l><r>rallegrare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# the pacifist letter, from Italian
# `per festeggiare un grande anniversario francese': to celebrate -- apertium-ita
# carries `festeggiare' and the bilingual pairs it with nothing. Read only into
# English (r="RL"), so `celebrates' is still written `celebra': `se celebrarán
# las elecciones' is no party
<e r="RL"><p><l>celebrate<s n="vblex"/></l><r>festeggiare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `nella cronaca dell'"Assalto a Palazzo Farnese"': the assault -- the
# dictionary has `assalto' only as what `assaltare' says of oneself. Read
# only into English, so `assault' is still written `aggressione'
<e r="RL"><p><l>assault<s n="n"/></l><r>assalto<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# `si batteva contro tutte le guerre': fought against them -- apertium-ita
# carries `battere' and the bilingual pairs it with nothing. `battersi' is
# the verb beating itself, which Spanish says as `batirse'; read only into
# English, so `beats' is still written `picchia'
<e r="RL"><p><l>beat<s n="vblex"/></l><r>battere<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `un certo pacifismo inetto': inept -- carried by apertium-ita, paired with
# nothing
<e><p><l>inept<s n="adj"/></l><r>inetto<s n="adj"/></r></p></e>
# the column on the mobile telephone, into Italian: `el telefonino
# destripado' is gutted, which apertium-ita carries as `sbudellare' and the
# bilingual pairs with nothing
<e><p><l>gut<s n="vblex"/></l><r>sbudellare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
# `los publicistas de las marcas': the dictionary's `pubblicista' is a
# freelance journalist, and the people who write advertising are
# `pubblicitari' -- read both ways, because an entry read only from English
# comes after the journalist, whose entry is read into English too
<e><p><l>publicist<s n="n"/></l><r>pubblicitario<s n="n"/><s n="GD"/></r></p></e>
# `su poseedor es persona ocupada': its owner -- apertium-ita carries
# `possessore' and the bilingual pairs it with nothing, so a sentence about
# one was refused for a word the target lacked
<e><p><l>possessor<s n="n"/></l><r>possessore<s n="n"/><s n="m"/></r></p></e>
# `la posibilidad ... de acentuar la individualidad de los ciudadanos':
# apertium-ita carries no verb for `stress', so the sentence was refused for
# a word the target lacked. `sottolineare' is what Italian says, written only
# (r="LR"), because read back it is still `underline'
<e r="LR"><p><l>stress<s n="vblex"/></l><r>sottolineare<s n="vblex"/></r></p></e>
